@prefix courier: <http://example.org/orexis/courier#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/courier#> .
@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .

courier: a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "the courier grid" ;
    rdfs:comment """A second planning domain, and the first whose distance is a NUMBER.

Hanoi proved a domain can be a plug-in; what it could not show is a search that needs
guiding, because its want is binary — every world scores 1.0 until one scores 0.0, so
nothing can be nearer. Here a want can be nearer: a van two cells from a parcel is closer
than one five cells away, and Manhattan distance says so in the same units a move costs.

That makes this world the case for an admissible heuristic. It is also the first domain
whose effects DELETE: picking a parcel up removes where it was and puts nothing in its
place, which every shipped effect until now has avoided by replacing a value instead of
removing one.""" .

#################  What the grid is  #################

courier:Cell a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "a place on the grid" ;
    rdfs:comment "Authored by a world, with coordinates. Cells are the world's rather than the domain's — unlike Hanoi's three pegs, how big the grid is IS the puzzle." .

courier:x a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain courier:Cell ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "column" .
courier:y a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain courier:Cell ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "row" ;
    rdfs:comment "Coordinates rather than a declared adjacency, because the heuristic needs to measure a distance the graph does not state. Two cells are neighbours where |dx| + |dy| = 1, which the move's own precondition works out." .

courier:Van a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "what moves, and what carries" ;
    rdfs:comment "One per world here. It has no capability and no grant: like Hanoi's mover, it is a pure subject of the search." .

courier:Parcel a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "a thing with somewhere to be" .

courier:destination a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain courier:Parcel ; rdfs:range courier:Cell ;
    rdfs:label "where this parcel is owed" ;
    rdfs:comment "AUTHORED and never moved — the world states it, and no effect touches it. It is what the want is measured against." .

#################  What is true at runtime  #################

courier:at a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range courier:Cell ;
    rdfs:label "where a van or an unloaded parcel stands" ;
    rdfs:comment "Runtime, seeded by whoever poses the puzzle, exactly as a reading is. Functional for a van, and ABSENT for a parcel being carried — which is the point: a pick REMOVES this, and no effect in this repository had ever removed a fact without putting another in its place." .

courier:carriedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain courier:Parcel ; rdfs:range courier:Van ;
    rdfs:label "aboard" ;
    rdfs:comment "The other half of the deletion: a parcel is at a cell OR aboard a van, never both and never neither." .

#################  What wanting a delivery MEANS — the domain's, not a world's  #################
#
#  A world asserts the want and points here. Both used to be written inline in
#  `world/courier/desire.ttl`, beside the want, and that was the wrong home: what "delivered"
#  means and how far away it is are courier knowledge, a second courier world would have had
#  to copy both, and the estimate is a PROMISE about this package's own actions — that it never
#  overstates what Drive, Pick and Drop will cost to finish — which only whoever declares those
#  costs can keep. The desire owns the term (`orexis:metWhen`, `orexis:estimates` hang off the
#  want); the package owns the measure. The shape is compiled by the kernel; the estimate is
#  run by it against whichever world is being judged, which the rule never names (#666).

courier:delivered a sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:label "every parcel standing where it is owed" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a want to deliver MEANS, said positively: on every parcel, the cell it is at equals the cell it is owed at. A carried parcel is at no cell, so its `courier:at` values are none and the equality fails — carrying it past the door is not delivering it. The kernel compiles this into the select whose rows are the parcels astray (#497); nobody writes that negation by hand any more. The object of a want's orexis:metWhen." ;
    sh:targetClass courier:Parcel ;
    sh:property [ sh:path courier:at ; sh:equals courier:destination ] .

courier:drivesOwed sh:select """
        SELECT (SUM(?d) AS ?estimate) WHERE {
            SELECT ?p (MIN(?dist) AS ?d) WHERE {
                {
                    ?p a courier:Parcel ; courier:destination ?owed .
                    ?owed courier:x ?ox ; courier:y ?oy .
                     ?p courier:carriedBy ?van . ?van courier:at ?vc .
                    ?vc courier:x ?vx ; courier:y ?vy .
                    BIND(ABS(?vx - ?ox) + ABS(?vy - ?oy) AS ?dist)
                } UNION {
                    ?p a courier:Parcel ; courier:destination ?owed .
                    ?owed courier:x ?ox ; courier:y ?oy .
                     ?p courier:at ?pc . ?v courier:at ?vc .
                    ?v a courier:Van .
                    ?pc courier:x ?px ; courier:y ?py .
                    ?vc courier:x ?vx ; courier:y ?vy .
                    FILTER(?px != ?ox || ?py != ?oy)
                    BIND(ABS(?vx - ?px) + ABS(?vy - ?py)
                         + ABS(?px - ?ox) + ABS(?py - ?oy) AS ?dist)
                }
            } GROUP BY ?p
        }""" ;
    rdfs:label "drives still owed" ;
    rdfs:comment """How far a delivery still is, in the unit a drive costs: Manhattan distance, because the grid is four-connected and a drive moves one cell — aboard the van it is the road to the door, standing somewhere it is the road to the parcel and then the parcel's road home. It counts drives and ignores the pick and the drop, so it NEVER OVERSTATES what is left, which is the promise the search ends on. The object of a want's orexis:estimates.

EACH BRANCH REPEATS THE PREAMBLE, and the repetition is not clumsiness: a UNION's branches are evaluated on their own and joined with the surrounding pattern afterwards, so a BIND inside a branch cannot see a variable bound outside it. Written the tidy way, ?ox and ?oy were unbound where the arithmetic ran and the aggregate came back 0 for every world — a heuristic that said every world was already home.""" .


#  WHAT THIS PACKAGE'S ACTIONS ARE FILLED WITH. One property per parameter, named in this
#  package's own words: the local part is the variable a precondition projects, the `$token`
#  its rules read and the predicate a planned step is written under. The kernel holds a row
#  to the list and reads none of them.

:van a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:label "van" ;
    rdfs:comment "The van a Drive moves, and the van a Pick loads into. Declared as orexis:takes by both." .

:parcel a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:label "parcel" ;
    rdfs:comment "The parcel a Pick loads or a Drop sets down." .

:to a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:label "to" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a Drive drives or a Drop drops — a cell of the grid." .

